.TH TX\-ENVELOPE 1 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.1.0" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
tx\-envelope \- common signing envelope (public half). Reads one JSON object from stdin
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
tx\-envelope [\-\-json OBJ]: common signing envelope (decode|verify|check|chain_table); public data only
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
(or `\-\-json '{...}'`) and prints one JSON object: decode / verify / check / chain_table
across UTXO, EVM, ZEC, Sol and Tron. Never takes a private key. Same schema as the C ABI
`bt_envelope` and `blocktools::envelope::run` (`envelope":"blocktools.sign/v1"`).
.nf
.RS 2
  echo '{"op":"decode","chain":"btc","tx":"<hex|psbt>"}' | tx\-envelope
  tx\-envelope \-\-json '{"op":"chain_table","chain":"_"}'
.RE
.fi
.SH WARNING
DANGEROUS: use at your own risk. Code that handles keys and signs transactions should always be read and audited by you before you trust it with real funds. Used incorrectly, it can destroy your money.
.br
Used incorrectly (wrong address, huge fee, lost keys, wrong chain) you lose funds; verify every transaction.
.br
It opens hostile wallet files and extracts secrets: run it only sealed and offline (./blocktools run).
.br
Unit tests are evidence, not a substitute for your own review. See docs/SECURITY.md.
.SH SEE ALSO
blocktools(7), docs/SECURITY.md, recipes/README.md
